tavily.snippet:
By replacing `%y` with a high-complexity division instruction, we avoid the operand reordering. This pattern is so common in `InstCombine` tests that it has its own term of art: thwarting.

The next thing to test are multi-use patterns: [...] The rule of thumb for `InstCombine` is that transforms should not increase the number of instructions. There are exceptions to this rule (for example, replacing a `udiv`/`sdiv` with a sequence of cheap instructions is fine), but most transforms satisfy this condition. [...] Interestingly, while we want to ultimately influence `InstCombine` behavior, the code change ended up in `ValueTracking`. The actual `InstCombine` fold that will ultimately make use of it are these two lines in the `visitAdd()` function of InstCombineAddSub.cpp:

```
// A+B -> A|B iff A and B have no bits set in common. if (haveNoCommonBitsSet(LHS, RHS, DL, &AC, &I, &DT)) return BinaryOperator::CreateOr(LHS, RHS);
```

